00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.490
Thanks to brilliant for sponsoring this video

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:06.930
Hi, I'm Toby and welcome back to another video today

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.809
I would like to show you through one of the most influential

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:15.689
PhD theses ever written and that is the work of Marie Curie. In this thesis

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.159
Marie talks about the discovery of the new elements radium and polonium

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.180
and also describes how she gained one of the first understandings of the new physical phenomenon of

00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:32.549
radioactivity. It was for that understanding that she was awarded the Nobel Prize in

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:40.739
1903 the same year this thesis was published and it was a few years later that she gained another Nobel Prize for the discovery

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:46.919
of those new elements this thesis was published at the University of Sorbonne in Paris

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:53.789
When Marie was 35 and she was the first woman in France to be awarded a doctoral degree

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:56.609
Although she was originally from Poland

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:01.199
So with that, let's have a little look at this really important work

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.479
The title of the thesis is research on radioactive substances

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:11.669
And it says it's by madam Sklodowska Curie, which is her full name

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:17.309
And in fact Marie is the French version of her original name Maria

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:20.159
This is a reprint of the thesis done in

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:24.240
1904 and this is of course not the original copy

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:30.150
the link is in the description to where I got this from the original documents would probably be quite

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:35.399
contaminated with radioactive substances, so I wouldn't want to be handling them.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.159
This is the table of contents and the original version was in French, but don't worry

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:43.529
I'll show you an English translation soon.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.709
This just shows us what some of the chapters were about

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.970
we have first an introduction a look at the history of the science then chapter one about the radioactivity of

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.170
uranium and thorium

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.090
which are radioactive

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.500
materials and

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.909
'radioactive' is actually a word that Marie used in this thesis

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:09.300
To first describe the phenomenon that she was seeing. She then talks about some new

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:17.589
radioactive substances that she's found and then the subsequent chapters talk about trying to separate those new radioactive substances

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:23.859
By some really tough chemical methods. Let's get page one of the English version over here

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:29.679
the introduction says that the work presented here has been worked on for the last four years and

00:02:30.109 --> 00:02:33.129
That she is being assisted by. Mr. Curie

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:41.120
That's pure her husband who has put aside his own work to help her on this new exciting field the work

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.360
establishes the existence of a new element radium.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:52.080
But the preparation of pure chloride of radium and the determination of the atomic weight of radium

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:58.899
form the main part of the work. This new element they have found has very curious properties and

00:02:59.299 --> 00:03:06.218
also a new method of chemical research is established. This work was kick-started by the discovery of

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:14.229
Röntgen rays, which we would call x-rays. These mysterious rays that could produce an image of the bones in your hand

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.800
caused a lot of excitement and around this time many new different kinds of mysterious rays were being reported.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:28.840
Some what kind of a false alarm but some were very legit. We have here that Mr. Becquerel has made similar

00:03:29.479 --> 00:03:32.049
experiments on the salts of uranium.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:39.099
This paragraph here explains that these penetrating uranium rays were not a result of

00:03:39.829 --> 00:03:45.939
Phosphorescence that means that even when the uranium compounds were placed in complete darkness

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.419
They still continued to act on photographic plates

00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:55.659
This was quite peculiar because at this time it was thought that for energy to be conserved

00:03:56.019 --> 00:04:02.739
The only way for light to be emitted from a material is for it to have been first absorbed

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:10.029
But if you're keeping your uranium in the dark and you're still seeing these rays then that doesn't seem to fit with the model

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:16.560
What it took many more years to realize is that energy is indeed being stored in these materials

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.960
But billions of years ago and stored in the form of mass

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.200
chapter one mentions that these uranium rays or Becquerel rays

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:30.409
cause a gas to become a feeble conductor of electricity

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.219
with that Marie develops a way to

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.339
measure the intensity of radiation of uranium by the effect of this radiation on the

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:44.100
Conductivity of air and down here is where she calls

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.660
substances, which generate Becquerel rays

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:54.420
radioactive. This is her experimental set up here where she measures the intensity of radiation

00:04:54.680 --> 00:05:00.440
What she does is to put a powder of her radioactive substance on this plate here

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.279
And then she applies a potential difference across these two plates

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:11.210
By setting up a circuit. The current in the circuit would be an indication of how well this

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.639
material is acting as a conductor and

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:20.719
therefore of how intense the radiation is. And the way that she got a measure of this current was having this

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.590
quartz crystal over here

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:29.960
Which is stressed by having some weights placed upon it. This crystal is an example of the piezoelectric effect

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.429
Which is when a stressed crystal will cause electricity to flow

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:39.379
Marie's husband Pierre actually discovered the piezoelectric effect in

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.070
1883

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:47.420
so he was really knowledgeable about how to use this in the circuit and what they did was to apply a

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.600
current that opposed the current in this circuit

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:55.640
and the amount of weight added to this plate here would be a measure of

00:05:56.070 --> 00:06:01.070
how strong a current was needed to fully oppose this conductivity

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:09.049
Marie thought that it would be improbable that radioactivity would be a property belonging to only one kind of matter

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:15.349
so she tested a variety of materials. This table contains the results of

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.200
uranium with a bunch of different uranium minerals. What is really unexpected and

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:27.169
important about these results is that there are a few uranium minerals that have a higher

00:06:27.689 --> 00:06:29.928
radioactivity than pure uranium.

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:36.588
This was a massive clue to the existence of a substance which was more radioactive than uranium.

00:06:37.769 --> 00:06:40.429
Considering this she is able to identify

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:44.449
two new strongly radioactive substances

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:49.369
polonium which she's named after her homeland of Poland and

00:06:50.249 --> 00:06:51.869
radium. They do a

00:06:51.869 --> 00:06:58.309
spectral analysis to confirm that these elements are different from elements that have already been studied

00:06:58.619 --> 00:07:01.849
But they haven't got a pure sample of these new

00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:09.618
elements yet and the thesis then moves on to using some pretty intense chemical methods to get pure

00:07:09.899 --> 00:07:15.768
samples. A pure sample would be needed to measure things like the atomic weight and

00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:22.669
officially add these new substances to the periodic table. Here Marie writes that after working for

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:26.299
several years to try and isolate radium and polonium,

00:07:27.059 --> 00:07:33.618
she's only succeeded in isolating radium. She failed with polonium for a few reasons

00:07:34.259 --> 00:07:35.999
including the fact that

00:07:35.999 --> 00:07:42.949
polonium was only present in the uranium ore in very tiny trace amounts the method for

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:47.299
Isolating radium that she describes over the next few pages in the thesis

00:07:48.389 --> 00:07:53.629
Demonstrates that this was a gruelling task done on an enormous scale.

00:07:54.119 --> 00:07:59.208
It shows her courage and stamina in chemistry. On this page

00:07:59.208 --> 00:08:05.540
We see the description of beautiful crystals which have formed and that's one of only three or four

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.639
subjective aesthetic judgments

00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:09.829
Which have snuck their way in to the thesis.

00:08:10.229 --> 00:08:18.078
After starting with millions of grams of raw material, Marie ends up with a sample of radium chloride

00:08:18.269 --> 00:08:22.849
which is about 1/10 of a gram. On this page here

00:08:22.849 --> 00:08:30.018
we see her finding the atomic weight of the sample and this is the purest sample here with only a trace of

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:35.088
Barium present. She lists the atomic weight as being 225

00:08:35.089 --> 00:08:41.359
And that's actually really close to the current accepted value of 226

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:47.569
That's really impressive for work that was done in a very humble shed

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:51.290
using very difficult raw materials and

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.290
chemical processes that had never been done before

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:59.869
All to understand a mysterious property that no one had even measured before

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.420
Marie mentions that for radium the strength of the radioactivity is

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:10.550
On the order of 10^6 higher than that of uranium

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:16.129
And again, this is in the correct magnitude of our currently accepted numbers

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.309
And again is a really great result.

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:24.590
I found it interesting that Marie did know about the different kinds of radiation

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:33.050
Alpha, beta and gamma rays as described by Ernest Rutherford around the same time and she has a little diagram here

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:40.580
showing these three different rays, that the gamma are the most penetrating, that alpha would be stopped after only a short

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:43.100
distance and that these have

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.939
opposite effect when acted on by a magnetic field.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.230
They didn't know what these types of radiation were

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:57.319
And in fact, the Curie's are trying to formulate an explanation of their radioactivity

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:04.969
Almost a decade before the atomic nucleus would be discovered. Another page from the thesis that I found interesting

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:07.369
was this one about the physiological

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:14.060
effects. Now it says here that radium rays exert an action upon the epidermis

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:19.609
So they knew that radioactivity was having some kind of biological effect.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:24.139
You may know that Marie Curie ultimately died from a disease

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.950
likely caused by her exposure to radiation and that

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:33.020
Made me think that they had no idea of the effect of radiation on the body

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:37.000
Perhaps it is true that they didn't know this effect would be detrimental

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:42.700
But the experiments on this page at least show that they knew it affected the skin.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.460
In one experiment Mr. Curie

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:51.060
caused a relatively weak radioactive product to act upon his arm for ten hours.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:58.380
The redness appeared immediately and later a wound was caused which took four months to heal.

00:10:59.089 --> 00:11:06.429
She then talks about some use of radioactive materials in the hospital for probably the treatment of

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:13.719
cancers and tumors. And actually that became something very useful about these properties and we still do use

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:19.300
Radiation to treat cancers. So when used carefully it can actually save lives

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:23.360
But we're not treated carefully it can also cost you lives.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:29.260
Chapter four moves on to explaining what was ultimately a wrong hypothesis that

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:31.700
radioactivity could be

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.819
communicated from one substance to another

00:11:34.339 --> 00:11:41.469
this idea was caused by a few of their strange results, maybe explained by the fact that their

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:47.589
Samples were disappearing by radioactive decay or being absorbed into the glass

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:54.729
Radium is produced by the decay of uranium. And that is why they are always found together

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:59.049
But Marie never commented on this fact of why these two

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:00.640
seemingly different

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.520
elements would always be found together and maybe if she had

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.720
it would have been more of a clue to explaining what was really going on.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.160
We will finish by going through Marie's

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:17.200
conclusions. The only sentence that she chooses to put in italics is that

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:20.109
Radium is a new chemical element

00:12:20.329 --> 00:12:27.369
So she was really proud of this discovery of a new element and that is what she is often known for today

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:31.140
It's true that this is a very impressive contribution

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:39.360
But even more so is their discovery of the property of radioactivity and she sums it up at the bottom here

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:45.480
our researchers upon the new radioactive bodies have given rise to a

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:46.940
scientific movement

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:51.540
Indeed. This would be the starting point for a lot more science and a lot more

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:58.840
Discoveries about the nature of the atom, why radiation occurs and how we can use it.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.160
So that has been a look at Marie Curie's work

